<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Neuwied</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Neuwied is a town on the right bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>. Founded in 1653 by Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-wied">Frederick III of Wied</a>, it grew as a planned residence town that welcomed religious minorities, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Brethren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>. It was famous for the Roentgen furniture workshop and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> in 1815.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/neuwied.jpg" alt="Neuwied" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frila · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fernblick.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Neuwied lies on the east bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a>, 12 km north-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a>, in the north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland-palatinate">Rhineland-Palatinate</a>. The railway from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankfurt">Frankfurt</a> am Main to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a> runs through it. One of the largest Roman castra on the Rhine has been excavated nearby, and Caesar's Rhine bridges are believed to have been built in the area. The inner town stands on a former bed of the Rhine and is at great risk of flooding, so it is one of the very few towns in the region protected by flood-prevention levees.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-iii-of-wied">Frederick III of Wied</a> founded Neuwied in 1653 as a fortress on the site of the village of Langendorf, destroyed in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years' War</a>. It was meant to be the new residence of the lower county, to secure its only access to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhine">Rhine</a> and to let the impoverished state share in Rhine trade. Because the flood-prone site attracted few settlers, the counts of Wied, especially Frederick III, Frederick William and John Frederick Alexander, granted self-administration and extensive <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/religious-tolerance">religious tolerance</a> there. Religious refugees came from other German territories, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/switzerland">Switzerland</a>, and from the middle of the 18th century Calvinists, Lutherans, Catholics, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mennonites">Mennonites</a>, Inspirationalists, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Brethren</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a> all lived in the town.</p><p>The newcomers helped the economy to rise in the 18th and 19th centuries. The cabinetmakers Abraham and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-roentgen">David Roentgen</a>, who belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravian-church">Moravian Brethren</a>, and the Mennonite clockmaker Peter Kinzing sent work to courts between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/versailles">Versailles</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>, and John Frederick Alexander's Rasselstein iron rolling mill made Neuwied one of the first industrial locations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. On 18 April 1797 French forces under General Louis Lazare Hoche defeated the Austrians under General Franz von Werneck at the Battle of Neuwied.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The town is the capital of the district of Neuwied and has 13 suburban districts, the largest being Heimbach-Weis. Its population grew from 5,600 in 1831 to 18,000 in 1905 with industrialisation, and reached 63,000 after several communities were incorporated. Neuwied is the native town of the paternal ancestors of John D. Rockefeller, whose family name is traced to the abandoned village of Rockenfeld, now within the municipal territory, and the birthplace of William of Wied, who briefly held the title of King of Albania in 1914. It is twinned with the London Borough of Bromley, Drom HaSharon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustrow">Güstrow</a> and Suqian.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Neuwied” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Count Frederick III of Wied founded the town of <b>Neuwied</b> on the Rhine in 1653, inviting settlers with promises of religious freedom.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wied-neuwied">County of Wied-Neuwied</a></li></ul></section>
